## Introduction
The vast topological, reticular, and compositional tunability, the crystallographically well-defined periodicity, and the chemically tailorable, molecular-scale porosity of metalorganic frameworks $(\mathsf{M O F s})^{1-3}$ make them attractive for a broad range of potential applications.4 Increasingly these applications extend to ones requiring electrical conductivity5,6- 8 for example, electrochromic switching,9-10 electrocatalysis,11-16 photoelectrode sensitization,17-21 resistivity-based chemical sensing,22-23 and perhaps even silicon-semiconductor-inspired microelectronic applications.24 As most MOFs are electrical insulators, a worthy fundamental challenge is to render frameworks electrically conductive.25 While multiple approaches have been taken, the two most prevalent are based on formation of electron-donor/electronacceptor complexes,26-29 or on mixed-valency, typically involving readily oxidized or readily reduced organic linkers.16, 28, 30-31
Here we focus on linker mixed-valency as the basis for MOF conductivity. We show that: a) the redox conductivity of the MOF NU-1000 is substantially anisotropic, b) the degree of anisotropy agrees with computational predictions, and c) depending on crystallite orientation, the magnitude of the redox conductivity ranges from somewhat less than needed to support typical (photo) electrocatalysis scenarios to clearly more-than-sufficient.
Our experimental focus is on carboxy-terminated tetraphenylpyrene and its radical cation, $\mathrm{TPPy^{+/0}}$ , as $\mathrm{TPPy}(\mathrm{COO^{-}})_{4}$ is the linker in the widely studied MOF, NU-1000; see Figure 1. Its csq topology renders the strength of linker-tolinker electronic coupling strongly dependent on coupling direction and different in the $^{a,b}$ plane than in the $c$ direction (hexagonal and trigonal channel direction). In the weakcoupling limit – achieved here by using redox-inert, hexazirconium(IV)oxy clusters as nodes – moveable charges are localized on individual linkers and conductivity is achieved by repetitive redox hopping. At the molecular scale, redox hopping is equivalent to electron self-exchange, albeit with coupling to movement of charge-balancing ions:
The hopping kinetics and dynamics can be broadly understood via the non-adiabatic version of Marcus’ theory of electron transfer.32-33 Thus, hopping rates are anticipated to scale as $t^{2}$ where $t$ is the donor:acceptor (linker:linker+) electronic coupling strength.
At the macroscopic level (and in the absence of a significant external electric field), redox-hopping can be modelled as charge diffusion. The electrochemically measurable diffusion coefficient, $D_{h o p p i n g}\left(\mathrm{cm}^{2}\:s^{-1}\right)$ , can be related to the microscopic redox-hopping or electron-exchange rate constant, $k_{h o p p i n g}\left(s^{-1}\right)$ , and the hopping distance, $\boldsymbol{r},$ via:
$$
k_{h o p p i n g}=\frac{D_{h o p p i n g}}{r^{2}}
$$
For TPPy sites in NU-1000, values for $r$ are $1.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ (center-tocenter according to the NU-1000 crystal structure,34 along the c-direction), and $2.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ ${\bf\dot{\boldsymbol{a}}},b$ plane (see SI for details)).
As illustrated in Figure 1, three types of hops (labelled $\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}_{1,}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}_{2},$ and ${\mathtt{n}}_{3}$ by Patwardan and Schatz)35 are possible. To move through the $^{a,b}$ plane, charges must execute both $\mathfrak{n}_{1}\mathfrak{-}$ and $\mathbf{n}_{2}.$ 一 type hops, while charge diffusion in the $c$ direction requires only ${\bf n}_{3}$ -type hops. From Patwardan and Schatz’ computations, pyrene-to-pyrene electronic coupling is mediated by phenyl substituents and, ultimately, orbital overlap for neighbouring carboxylates, with essentially no contribution from intervening $\operatorname{zr}$ ions; see Figure 2. The computations show that $t_{3}{}^{2}{\approx}t_{1}{}^{2}{>}{>}t_{2}{}^{2}$ . The differences translate to a predicted factor of ${\sim}3000$ for charge-diffusion in the $c$ direction versus within the $^{a,b}$ -plane; see SI and ref. 15.
Computational work by Patwardan and Schatz has shown that electron-density that relevant to coupling extends throughout the linker, including the phenyl groups (grey and white) and terminal carboxylate oxygen atoms (red). Note that for all three types of coupling, carboxylate oxygens from neighbouring linkers are held proximal by a single $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ atom (green). Nevertheless, the distances between the oxygen atoms comprising an electronic-coupling-relevant pair are smaller for $t_{1}$ and $t_{3}$ type coupling, than for $t_{2}$ coupling. The differences are perhaps easiest to see by comparing $0{-}\mathrm{{Zr{-}}}0$ angles, as shown in the figure. While other factors may be involved, a striking consequence of the small differences in siting geometry for the various pairs of electronically coupled linkers translate to ca. 90-fold differences in coupling energy, and ca. 8,000-fold differences in $t^{2}.$ , the parameter relevant for determining relative rates of $\mathrm{TPPy^{+/0}}$ based electron self-exchange.
## Results and Discussion
To quantify directional charge-transport we turned to electrochemical measurements of MOFs in thin-film form (microns), on fluorine-doped tin-oxide (FTO) electrodes. We have shown elsewhere that thin-films of NU-1000 can be grown directly on metal-oxides via solvothermal chemistry.36 Prior to MOF formation, we added (via atomic layer deposition) several angstroms of a metal-oxide $(\mathrm{ZnO})^{37}$ that is insulating over the electrochemical range of interest. The purpose here is to suppress unwanted current due to oxidation of residual water in the electrolyte solution, while still permitting electrons to readily tunnel38 between the FTO and MOF.
MOF films can also be obtained via electrophoretic deposition (EPD) of suspended crystallites (hexagonal rods) on $z\mathrm{n}0$ coated FTO substrates.39-40 The solvothermal approach yields crystallites (also hexagonal rods) that are oriented at a tilt to the electrode surface with one end of the rod grafted to electrode.36 In contrast, EPD yields films consisting of crystallites oriented mainly parallel to the electrode, with contact made via one of the hexagonal faces; see Figures 3 and 4, respectively,  for schematic and experimental illustrations (scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements). (For simplicity, and to obviate potential concerns about charge transport across MOF grain boundaries, we limited electrophoretic deposition to roughly a monolayer of crystallites.) Both kinds of films were characterized by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and the observed patterns are in accordance with the simulated powder XRD pattern of NU1000 (Figure S2). (See SI for synthesis, fabrication, and characterization details To extract details of the crystallite orientation, PXRD patterns of NU-1000 were simulated using the March-Dollase formula: $\mathrm{(r^{2}c o s^{2}(\alpha)\quad+\quad s i n^{2}(\alpha\alpha)/r)^{-3/2}}$ , where $r$ is related to the amount of preferred orientation and $\upalpha$ is the angle between the normal to the plane of a reflection (001).41 (For ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}}}=1$ , the orientations are random.) The (100), (001) intensity ratio in the experimental PXRD pattern of EPD-NU-1000 is reproduced in a simulated pattern with $\mathbf{r}<$ 1, indicating that the (001) planes are preferentially oriented parallel to the FTO substrate. In contrast, solvothermal-NU1000 films ratio of peak intensities is reproduced in a simulated pattern with $\mathrm{~\boldmath~r~}>\ 1$ , indicating that the (100) planes are preferentially oriented parallel to the FTO substrate.42
Shown in Figure 5 are cyclic voltammograms (CVs) of MOF films. Evident are peaks attributable to the MOF-based $\mathrm{TPPy^{+/0}}$ couple.43 Visible-region electrochromic spectra (not shown), especially for the more rapidly oxidizable solvothermal-grown version of the thin-film material, support the assignment of peaks as the reversible electro-oxidation of the MOFcoordinated TPPy to its cation radical form.10, 40 Note that beyond the first few linker layers, electrochemical oxidation of a film necessarily entails redox hopping between linkers – either along the $c$ direction (solvothermal films; $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{n}_{3}\dot{}.}\end{array}$ ) or through the $^{a,b}$ plane (EPD films; $\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{n}_{1}+\mathsf{n}_{2}\overline{{\vphantom{\frac{1}{2}}}}.}\end{array}$ ).
For interface-immobilized redox couples, a non-zero difference in anodic and cathodic peak potentials is qualitative evidence for diffusion control of charge transport, as is tailing the voltammetric waves. Both effects are considerably more prominent for EPD-prepared films, implying slower charge transport. Also evident are differences in anodic vs. cathodic voltammetric wave shape (see panel $a$ ) – behavior that can be caused, for example, by coupling of redox events to chargebalancing uptake or release of ions from the external electrolyte.44
Charge-transport anisotropy can be quantified by comparing values for $D_{h o p p i n g}$ in the $c$ direction (channel-aligned direction) versus through the $^{a,b}$ -plane.  Experimental values can be obtained by applying the Cottrell equation to the initial portions of current vs. inverse of square-root of time signals generated by stepping the electrode potential across the formal potential for $\mathrm{TPPy^{+/0}}$ ; see Supplemental Information for details. Figure 5 (panels C and D) shows representative plots, while Table 1 summarizes numerical results. Values for Dhopping range from $<10^{-12}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace s^{-1}$ to nearly $10^{-10}\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}{\mathrm{s}}^{{\mathrm{-1}}}$ . Most notably, redoxhopping-based charge transport, as represented by $D_{h o p p i n g}$ is 300-fold faster in the MOF crystallite c direction than through the $^{a,b}$ -plane – results that are broadly consistent with the computational predictions.35,45
Related to, but not identical to, charge-diffusion type analysis of redox hopping within MOF films is assessment of MOF electrical conductivity () via EIS (electrochemical impedance spectroscopy). EIS entails evaluating the real and imaginary components of the impedance to the flow of current in response to small-amplitude perturbations of applied voltage, over a wide range of perturbation frequencies. EIS has the virtue of separating and reporting on processes occurring on differing timescales, i.e. in different frequency domains. Electrical conductivities of films were estimated by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) at an applied potential of $1.6\:\mathrm{V}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ ; the solvothermal NU-1000 films showed 100 times higher electrical conductivity $[1.2\mathrm{~x~}10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) compared to EPD-NU-1000 films $(1.3\mathrm{x}10^{-9}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}})$ (Figure S4).Thus, these measurements corroborate the finding of sizable anisotropy in electrochemical potential-step experiments.
Returning to Table 1 and Figure 5c, a small but readily observable difference in $D_{h o p p i n g}$ exists for measurements derived from oxidative vs. reductive potential steps – the oxidative value being ${\sim}50\%$ larger. Since only the initial portions of chronoamperometry curves are used for assessing $D_{h o p p i n g},$ the hopping environment following a 0 to $1.6~\mathrm{V}$ step approximates that shown in eq. 1a, i.e. few chargecompensating ions are present, while for values obtained by stepping from 1.6 to $0\mathrm{~V~}_{\l}$ , the hopping environment is rich in charge-compensating ions. The difference in values for the diffusion coefficient points to a kinetically significant role for coupled movement of ions in response to electron (or hole) transfer in MOFs – a role that conceivably could be brought to the fore by ion-pairing (electrolyte-anion/linker cation) in the low-dielectric solvent, $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (dielectric constant, $\varepsilon=8.9$ ).46
With this notion in mind, chronoamperometry experiments were also run in acetonitrile (AN) as solvent $\left(\varepsilon=37\right)$ , with 1 M tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate $\left(\mathrm{TBAPF}_{6}\right)$ ) as electrolyte. In this medium, $D_{h o p p i n g}$ in the MOF crystallite $c$ direction is 3,500-fold faster than through the $^{a,b}$ -plane;47 see Figure S7 and Table S2. Thus, the ratio is in very close – perhaps fortuitously close – agreement with theory, where theory does not consider the possible effects of ion-pairing or the distinction between eqs. 1a and 1b.
For EIS assessment of electrical conductivity $\upsigma$ and for potential-step measurements of $D_{h o p p i n g},$ we initially used $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{\mathrm{^{-}}}$ as the electrolyte anion. With a diameter of ${\mathfrak{s}}\mathrm{\AA},$ this anion is sufficiently small to navigate the hexagonal and trigonal channels of NU-1000 (widths of ${\sim}30$ and $1{\overset{\circ}{2}}{\overset{\delta}{\mathrm{A}}}_{\mathrm{.}}$ , respectively), as well as the ca. $8\textup{\AA}$ diameter apertures interconnecting them. At the suggestion of a reviewer, we extended the studies to films in contact with, and infiltrated by, acetonitrile solutions of sodium tetrakis[3,5-bis(trifluoromethyl)phenyl]borate, also known as ${\mathrm{Na^{+}B A R F}}$ . Note that with a diameter of ${15\textup{\AA}},$ BARF(see drawing) can enter, exit, and move within NU-1000 only via the material’s hexagonal channels.  For solvothermal films, these channels are oriented in the same direction (c direction) as used for assessment of MOF electrical conductivity and redox hopping. For EPD films, the hexagonal channels are oriented largely parallel to the underlying electrode and orthogonal to the direction used for assessment of electrical conductivity and redox-hopping. Thus, comparisons of results obtained with $\mathrm{PF}_{6}{\mathrm{^{-}}}$ versus BARF- as the electrolyte anion should provide further insight into the role of charge-compensating ions in anisotropically propagating electrons.
Full results are presented in the Supporting Information. Briefly, however, for EPD films neither $D_{h o p p i n g}$ or $\upsigma$ is sensitive to the size of the charge-compensating anion. As noted above, EPD films yield considerably smaller values than do solvothermal films, for $D_{h o p p i n g}$ and $\upsigma$ – results consistent (Figure 2) with poorer linker-linker electronic coupling in the crystal $^{a,b}$ -plane than along the $c$ -direction.  In contrast, for solvothermal films, replacing the compact hexafluorophosphate ion with the much larger BARF anion engenders two- to four-fold decreases in $D_{h o p p i n g}$ and $\upsigma$ . The Nyquist plots of impedance data in Figure 6 illustrate the contrasting sensitivity of EPD and solvothermal films to electrolyte composition. Further study will be needed to understand the microscopic basis for the contrasting sensitivity.
We and others have shown that reaction rates (currents) for reasonably electrocatalytic MOF films of micron thickness are charge-transport limited when $D_{h o p p i n g}\leq10^{-13}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1};1^{2}$ 2, 16, 48-49 if catalysis requires participation of active-sites across the full thickness of micron-thick MOF film, catalytic currents of less than $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{2}$ are supported. In contrast, under the described conditions, $D_{h o p p i n g}$ values of $10^{-11}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace s^{-1}$ are anticipated to be capable of supporting catalytic currents of up to tens or hundreds of mA $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . An oft-quoted target for photoelectrochemical reactions, at an intensity of one sun, is 10 $\mathbf{mAcm^{-2}}$ .
For an electrocatalyst-functionalized version of the anisotropic material studied here, charge transport through the $^{a,b}$ -plane would fail to reach the $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ target for catalytic current; or, for electrocatalysts having sufficiently large TOFs (turnover frequencies), would reach the target only by using a small fraction of the film. A consequence of using only a fraction of the immobilized electrocatalysts should be an increase in kinetic overpotential – thereby negating one of the key advantages of employing MOFs as electrocatalyst supports.
## Conclusions
In summary, we find that redox-hopping based chargetransport in NU-1000 is highly anisotropic, with values for $D_{h o p p i n g}$ along the direction of the MOF’s trigonal & hexagonal channels exceeding by ${\sim}200\ –$ to 3,500-fold those for transport in the $^{a,b}$ -plane. EIS measurements show that NU-1000’s electrical conductivity is similarly anisotropic. The various measurements are facilitated by the complementary parallel and normal orientations of hexagonal-rod-shaped crystallites immobilized on planar electrodes by via electrophoretic and solvothermal methods, respectively. The observed anisotropy is in good qualitative agreement with the predictions of previous computational work where linker-to-linker electronic coupling, in the nonadiabatic limit, was identified as the governing factor. The transport anisotropy is a direct consequence of the reduced symmetry of NU-1000 in comparison, for example, to MOFs that are describable using scu (simple cubic, e.g., MOF-5) or nbo (e.g., HKUST-1) topologies.
The obtained values for $D_{h o p p i n g}$ span the range from less than sufficient to support full participation of a micron-thick MOFbased electrocatalyst at a benchmark current density of $10\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , to more than sufficient to satisfy this requirement. Since an electrocatalyst-supporting, porous MOF film presents the equivalent of roughly a thousand monolayers of molecular catalyst per micron of film thickness, a transport-optimized film can be expected to deliver catalytic current at substantially smaller kinetic overpotential (i.e., hundreds of millivolts) than the same catalyst present in solution at millimolar concentration. Thus, efficient charge-transport is essential for capitalizing on the overpotential minimization and limitingcurrent maximization promised by this comparatively new kind of electrocatalyst configuration.
While the example here features bidirectional anisotropy, one could easily envision frameworks that are anisotropically redox-conductive in three distinct directions. One could also envision MOF linkers and nodes that can be combined to yield substantially greater anisotropy. Finally, one could envision replicating or opposing the anisotropy for redox conductivity with anisotropy in molecular transport, excitonic energy transport, or other properties. We anticipate reporting on examples of such behaviour in due course, and on exploiting the combined properties in energy-conversion-related chemical applications.
